BLOCK|h1|1|Chapter 30: Zero-Glue C/C++ Interoperability & Native Math
BLOCK|p||A major barrier when adopting new systems programming languages is the "glue code problem": writing thousands of lines of wrappers, FFI bindings, Cython definitions, or PyO3 adapters to call existing C/C++ libraries.
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang solves this completely with **Zero-Glue C Integration**. You can directly include C standard library headers, link system shared libraries, and call native C functions with zero overhead and zero wrappers.
BLOCK|p||This chapter covers:
BLOCK|p||1. **The `c_include` Directive** for instant standard header ingestion.
BLOCK|p||2. **The `link` Directive & Compiler Linker Flags** (`-l<lib>`, `-L<dir>`).
BLOCK|p||3. **The `extern fn` Declaration** for custom C/C++ symbol signatures.
BLOCK|p||4. **Native Double & Floating-Point LLVM Lowering**.
BLOCK|p||5. **High-Performance Built-in Math Functions**.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|1. Direct Header Ingestion with `c_include`
BLOCK|p||To use standard C functions, simply include the header at the top of your Cluster source file:
BLOCK|code|python|c_include "math.h"
c_include "stdio.h"

fn main():
    # Call C standard library functions directly!
    x := 16.0
    s := sqrt(x)
    put "Square root of 16.0 is: " + to_text(s)
    
    val := cos(0.0)
    put "Cos(0.0) is: " + to_text(val)
BLOCK|p||The compiler automatically imports function prototypes, return types, and parameter signatures for headers like `math.h`, `stdio.h`, `stdlib.h`, `string.h`, `time.h`, and `unistd.h`.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|2. Dynamic Library Linking (`link`)
BLOCK|p||When your code depends on external C libraries (such as `libm`, `libcrypto`, `libcurl`, or `libsqlite3`), use the `link` directive:
BLOCK|code|python|# Instructs the linker to link libm (-lm)
link "m"

# Instructs the linker to link libssl (-lssl)
link "ssl"
BLOCK|p||You can also pass library names and library search paths directly from the CLI:
BLOCK|code|bash|./cl run -lm -L/usr/local/lib main.cl
BLOCK|p||The Cluster compiler automatically injects `-Wl,-rpath` flags to ensure your compiled binaries locate shared libraries at runtime without manual `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` configuration.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|3. External Function Declarations (`extern fn`)
BLOCK|p||For custom third-party C/C++ libraries that do not have automated header mappings, you can declare external functions using the `extern fn` syntax:
BLOCK|code|python|link "m"

# Declare external C signatures
extern fn hypot(x: float, y: float) -> float
extern fn pow(base: float, exponent: float) -> float

fn main():
    h := hypot(3.0, 4.0)
    put "Hypotenuse (3, 4) = " + to_text(h)    # 5.000000

    p := pow(2.0, 10.0)
    put "2^10 = " + to_text(p)                 # 1024.000000
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|4. Native 64-bit Float Lowering
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang features full native LLVM `double` lowering for IEEE 754 64-bit floating point numbers:
BLOCK|list||**Float Arithmetic**: Lowered to native CPU instructions (`fadd`, `fsub`, `fmul`, `fdiv`, `fneg`).
BLOCK|list||**Float Comparisons**: Evaluated via IEEE compliant ordered comparison (`fcmp oeq`, `fcmp one`, `fcmp olt`, `fcmp ogt`).
BLOCK|list||**Automatic Int-to-Float Promotion**: Passing integer values where floats are expected automatically inserts zero-cost `sitofp` conversions.
BLOCK|code|python|fn main():
    a := 3.14159
    b := 2.71828

    sum := a + b
    diff := a - b
    prod := a * b
    div := a / b

    put sum
    put diff
    put prod
    put div
BLOCK|hr||
